# Deviations from analysis_plan.md (plan-v1)

The initial operational and modelling decisions below were documented before any outcome model was fitted. Descriptive event counts by exposure category (results/event_counts.csv) were viewed before the models were specified. Subsequent entries document analysis implementation, diagnostic findings and any resulting decisions.

## 2026-11-17: Age variable for population definition and age adjustment

Plan: "Adults aged 20 years and older at the MEC examination".

Finding: RIDAGEEX (age at examination, in months) is missing for all 1,443 examined participants aged 80 years or older. In the pooled 2007-2014 demographics files it is also missing for 18,501 of 37,723 examined participants younger than 80.

Decision: use RIDAGEYR (age in years at the screening interview, top-coded at 80) for the age 20+ restriction and for the age spline.

Reason: RIDAGEYR is complete, whereas RIDAGEEX would drop the oldest participants and about half of the examined sample.

Consequences: age is measured at the screening interview rather than at examination (usually a difference of a few weeks). Top-coding at 80 limits resolution of age within the 80+ group. This will be noted as a limitation.

Scope: no change to the research question, exposures, outcome, adjustment sets or models.

## 2026-11-17: Operational definitions fixed before outcome linkage (no change to plan)

MORTSTAT and PERMTH_EXM were not examined at the time of these entries; only ELIGSTAT was linked.

- Race/ethnicity: RIDRETH1 (RIDRETH3 is not available in 2007-2010).
- Education: DMDEDUC2 in four categories; refused and don't know coded as missing.
- Income-poverty ratio: INDFMPIR categorised as <1.3, 1.3-3.49, >=3.5 (NHANES top-codes at 5).
- Physical activity: five domains from PAQ605-PAD675; if any domain is unknown (refused, don't know, or time set to missing by NCHS), total activity is missing. Gate answer "no" counts as 0 minutes. S2 cut-points as planned.
- Alcohol: ALQ101 = 9 or ALQ110 = 9 treated as missing, following the plan's lifetime-drinking rule (this includes 10 participants with ALQ101 = 9 and ALQ110 = 1). Codes 777 and 999 in ALQ120Q and ALQ130 treated as missing.
- Pregnancy: RIDEXPRG = 1 excluded; RIDEXPRG = 3 (cannot ascertain) retained.
- Participant flow: 22,673 examined adults aged 20+; 22,632 with ELIGSTAT = 1; 22,385 not pregnant; 18,155 complete cases (18.9% excluded). The 10% rule in the plan is therefore met, and multiple imputation will be run as a sensitivity analysis.
- Added limitations: public-use mortality files are perturbed by NCHS to reduce re-identification risk; linkage through 31 December 2019 is used as pre-specified, although NCHS has since updated linkage through 2022.

## 2026-11-17: Modelling details fixed before running any outcome model (no change to plan)

- Age spline: natural cubic spline with 4 df. Interior knots at the 25th, 50th and 75th percentiles of age in the primary complete-case population; boundary knots at its minimum and maximum (expected 20 and 80). The same basis is used in all models, including sensitivity analyses.
- Confidence intervals: Wald intervals using the t distribution with design degrees of freedom.
- Hazard ratios are reported only for the exposure of interest in each model.

## 2026-11-17: Implementation of the proportional hazards test (no change to plan)

The plan specifies exposure-by-log-follow-up-time interaction terms with design-based Wald tests (survey::regTermTest). To avoid relying on time-transform terms inside svycoxph, follow-up is split into 6-month episodes and each exposure is interacted with the log of the episode midpoint. Tests use the primary M3 adjustment sets and a joint Wald test of all interaction terms per exposure. Proportional hazards is considered not supported for an exposure if the joint p-value is below 0.05. In that case the pre-specified five-year split is applied and interval-specific hazard ratios are reported. Episode splitting leaves the partial likelihood unchanged; this is checked by reproducing the M3 smoking estimate on the split data.

## 2026-11-18: Correction to the proportional hazards test implementation (no change to plan)

The first implementation (run on 2026-11-17) interacted every exposure level, including the reference level, with log(time). Without a main effect of time this is not identifiable, and the models failed with a singular-matrix error before any estimate or p-value was produced. The corrected version includes interaction terms for the non-reference levels only. Adjustment sets, follow-up splitting, the joint Wald test and the decision rule (p < 0.05) are unchanged.

## 2026-11-18: Proportional hazards diagnostic results and activation of the pre-specified time-split analysis

The pre-specified proportional hazards diagnostics were implemented using exposure-by-log-follow-up-time interactions in survey-weighted Cox models. Follow-up was split into six-month episodes, and joint design-based Wald tests were used to assess the interaction terms.

Results:

- Smoking: F = 1.398, p = 0.2571.
- Alcohol: F = 0.287, p = 0.8345.
- Physical activity: F = 3.259, p = 0.0473.
- BMI: F = 0.971, p = 0.4165.

Decision: The physical activity model showed evidence against the proportional hazards assumption at the 0.05 significance level. Therefore, the pre-specified five-year time-split analysis will be applied to physical activity, with interval-specific hazard ratios reported. No time-split analysis is triggered by the current diagnostics for smoking, alcohol or BMI.

The six-month episode splitting used for the diagnostic models reproduced the original smoking M3 coefficients (coefficient comparison: TRUE).

Scope: This is an implementation of the pre-specified diagnostic and contingency procedure, not a change to the original analysis plan. The time intervals and estimation details for the five-year analysis are documented in the next entry.

## 2026-11-18: Five-year time-split analysis for physical activity

The pre-specified five-year split procedure was implemented for physical activity following the PH diagnostic result (joint interaction test p = 0.0473).

Follow-up was divided into two periods: 0-5 years and after 5 years. The period-specific survey-weighted Cox model included physical activity, interactions between physical activity categories and the period after five years, and the pre-specified physical activity M3 adjustment set.

Period-specific hazard ratios (reference: sufficient physical activity):

- Insufficient activity, 0-5 years: HR 1.392 (95% CI 1.091-1.775).
- Inactive, 0-5 years: HR 2.070 (95% CI 1.722-2.487).
- Insufficient activity, after 5 years: HR 1.217 (95% CI 0.988-1.500).
- Inactive, after 5 years: HR 1.662 (95% CI 1.408-1.962).

The joint Wald test of the two physical activity-by-period interaction terms was not statistically significant (F = 1.633; 2 and 47 df; p = 0.2062). This does not establish equality of the period-specific associations; it indicates insufficient evidence of a difference between the two periods in this model.

Outputs are saved in `results/pa_time_varying.csv`, `results/pa_time_varying_interaction_test.csv`, and `results/pa_time_varying_model_coefficients.csv`.

## 2026-11-18: Survey design construction in the PH and time-split analyses (no change to plan)

The plan specifies building the survey design before subsetting. The primary models (M1-M3) follow this (subset of the design built on all examined participants). The proportional hazards tests and the five-year time-split analysis were run on designs built from the complete-case rows only, because the episode-split data were created from the complete-case population. For the M3 smoking model, the design built on complete cases gave the same standard errors and the same design degrees of freedom as the subset of the full design (all.equal = TRUE). This equivalence was checked for that model only; the same construction was used for the other models.

## 2026-11-18: Multiple imputation specification fixed before running imputation (no change to plan)

- Population: eligible non-pregnant adults (n = 22,385).
- Imputed variables: smoking, alcohol, physical activity, BMI category, education and income-poverty ratio (polytomous regression, mice method "polyreg").
- Predictors: all other analysis variables (sex, race, age), the mortality event indicator, the Nelson-Aalen cumulative hazard, the examination weight, strata and PSU. Follow-up time enters only through the Nelson-Aalen estimate. Participant ID is not used.
- Settings: 50 imputed datasets, 10 iterations, cluster seed 20261118. Imputations are run in parallel (mice::parlmice) because one iteration takes about 1.5 minutes. Convergence is checked graphically.
- Analysis: primary M3 models only, fitted in each imputed dataset with the same survey design variables as the primary analysis, and pooled with Rubin's rules.

## 2026-11-19: Multiple imputation run (no change to plan)

The 50 imputations were run as specified (10 iterations, cluster seed 20261118) in parallel with mice::parlmice on 10 workers, using mice version 3.19.0. The run took 1.46 hours; no logged events; no missing values remained in the imputed variables. mice has since deprecated parlmice in favour of futuremice; the run is unaffected.

Pooled analysis: the primary M3 model for each exposure is fitted in each imputed dataset on the eligible non-pregnant population (n = 22,385) and pooled with Rubin's rules (mitools::MIcombine), with design degrees of freedom as the complete-data degrees of freedom. The age spline uses the same knots as the primary analysis. The design is built from the eligible rows rather than subsetted from the full design; because no PSU is lost, this is equivalent (design degrees of freedom are checked against the primary analysis).
Convergence was assessed graphically (chain means and standard deviations of the imputed values by iteration, 50 chains; results/mi_convergence_1.png and results/mi_convergence_2.png): no trend or divergence between chains was seen for the six imputed variables.